WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.200
Certain agent actions should require human approval before execution.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:16.020
Examples include access changes, financial transactions, sensitive communications, destructive operations, production changes, data sharing, or actions with legal or safety consequences.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:22.960
The required oversight should reflect the impact, reversibility, uncertainty, and sensitivity of the resource involved.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:27.420
An approval gate should provide meaningful context rather than a generic confirmation.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:35.560
The reviewer should see the exact proposed action, target, relevant data, reason, expected effect, and any important uncertainty.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:44.100
Approval must occur before execution, and the system should bind it to the reviewed parameters so the agent cannot silently substitute a different action afterward.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.040
Risk tiers help balance control with productivity.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:56.300
Reading public documentation, displaying help, or preparing a draft may not need the same review as sending, deleting, purchasing, merging, or changing access.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:04.680
Organizations can automate well-bounded, reversible tasks while placing deliberate human judgment at transitions where consequences become significant.

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:09.860
If approval is missing, denied, or expired, the workflow should stop safely.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.880
It should not seek another tool to bypass the decision or quietly reduce safeguards.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:19.580
Highly sensitive environments may require separation of duties or a second approver.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:25.640
Approval logs should record who reviewed what, when the decision occurred, and which exact action was permitted.
